- id: 111
  question: "Which field in a Pod spec allows you to define a lifecycle hook that runs after the container exits?"
  options:
    A: "postStart"
    B: "preStop"
    C: "postStop"
    D: "preStart"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "postStart runs before the container starts."
    B: "preStop is executed just before container termination. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/container-lifecycle-hooks/#container-hooks)"
    C: "postStop is not a Kubernetes hook."
    D: "preStart does not exist."

- id: 112
  question: "What does the 'spec.selector' field in a DaemonSet control?"
  options:
    A: "Which nodes the DaemonSet runs on"
    B: "Which pods the DaemonSet manages"
    C: "The Service that fronts the DaemonSet"
    D: "The label used for pod affinity"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "Node selection is handled by nodeSelector/affinity, not selector."
    B: "selector matches pod labels created by the DaemonSet, defining ownership. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/daemonset/#pod-management)"
    C: "DaemonSets do not create Services."
    D: "Affinity uses separate fields."

- id: 113
  question: "Which annotation enables automatic injection of the Linkerd data plane proxy into a pod?"
  options:
    A: "linkerd.io/inject: \"enabled\""
    B: "linkerd.io/inject: \"true\""
    C: "linkerd.io/auto-inject: \"enabled\""
    D: "linkerd.io/proxy: \"auto\""
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "Linkerd uses linkerd.io/inject: \"enabled\" to trigger injection. (https://linkerd.io/2.13/tasks/inject/)"
    B: "While true may work in some setups, the documented value is \"enabled\"."
    C: "Incorrect annotation key."
    D: "Invalid annotation."

- id: 114
  question: "What is the effect of setting 'spec.tolerations' with 'operator: Exists' and 'effect: NoExecute' on a pod?"
  options:
    A: "The pod can be scheduled on nodes with matching NoExecute taints and will not be evicted when the taint is added"
    B: "The pod can be scheduled on nodes with matching NoExecute taints but will be evicted after the tolerationSeconds expire"
    C: "The pod ignores all taints on nodes"
    D: "The pod will never be scheduled on tainted nodes"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "NoExecute tolerations allow scheduling but cause eviction after tolerationSeconds."
    B: "Operator Exists matches any key; with NoExecute the pod is evicted after the specified tolerationSeconds (or immediately if omitted). (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration/#taint-based-evictions)"
    C: "Only NoSchedule or PreferNoSchedule can be ignored; NoExecute still triggers eviction."
    D: "It permits scheduling, not prohibits it."

- id: 115
  question: "Which field in a Service spec determines whether the Service will allocate a stable virtual IP inside the cluster?"
  options:
    A: "type"
    B: "clusterIP"
    C: "externalIPs"
    D: "sessionAffinity"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "type defines how the Service is exposed (ClusterIP, NodePort, etc.)."
    B: "clusterIP set to a non‑None value allocates a stable ClusterIP. Setting it to \"None\" makes the Service headless. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#headless-services)"
    C: "externalIPs expose the Service outside the cluster."
    D: "sessionAffinity affects routing, not IP allocation."

- id: 116
  question: "What does the 'kubectl top pod' command require to be installed in the cluster?"
  options:
    A: "Metrics Server"
    B: "Heapster"
    C: "Prometheus Adapter"
    D: "Kube‑state‑metrics"
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "Metrics Server aggregates resource usage and powers 'kubectl top'. (https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server)"
    B: "Heapster is deprecated."
    C: "Adapter is for custom metrics, not core top."
    D: "Kube‑state‑metrics provides object state, not usage metrics."

- id: 117
  question: "Which of the following is a valid way to reference a ConfigMap key as an environment variable in a pod?"
  options:
    A: "env:\n  - name: MY_VAR\n    valueFrom:\n      configMapKeyRef:\n        name: my-config\n        key: my-key"
    B: "env:\n  - name: MY_VAR\n    value: \"$(my-config.my-key)\""
    C: "envFrom:\n  - configMapRef:\n      name: my-config"
    D: "command:\n  - \"$(MY_VAR)\""
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "Using valueFrom.configMapKeyRef correctly references a single key. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/environment-variable-expose-pod-information/#use-configmap-as-an-environment-variable)"
    B: "$(...) syntax is not interpreted for env values."
    C: "envFrom imports all keys, not a single key."
    D: "Command substitution is not performed by the container runtime."

- id: 118
  question: "What is the purpose of the 'spec.revisionHistoryLimit' field in a Deployment?"
  options:
    A: "Limit the number of old ReplicaSets retained for rollback"
    B: "Set the maximum number of Pods the Deployment can create"
    C: "Control how many times the Deployment can be updated per hour"
    D: "Define the number of times a failed rollout is retried"
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "revisionHistoryLimit caps stored old ReplicaSets, affecting rollback availability. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/#revision-history-limit)"
    B: "Replica count is controlled by spec.replicas."
    C: "No rate‑limit exists."
    D: "Retry behavior is governed by backoff settings, not this field."

- id: 119
  question: "Which field in a Service definition controls whether the Service forwards traffic to pods on the same node only?"
  options:
    A: "externalTrafficPolicy: Local"
    B: "sessionAffinity: ClientIP"
    C: "internalTrafficPolicy: Local"
    D: "loadBalancerSourceRanges"
  answer: C
  explanations:
    A: "externalTrafficPolicy affects traffic coming from outside the cluster."
    B: "sessionAffinity controls client‑IP stickiness, not node locality."
    C: "internalTrafficPolicy: Local ensures traffic stays on the same node when possible. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#internaltrafficpolicy)"
    D: "loadBalancerSourceRanges restrict external IP ranges for LoadBalancer services."

- id: 120
  question: "What does the 'kubectl apply -k <directory>' command do?"
  options:
    A: "Applies a single YAML file in the directory"
    B: "Recursively processes Kustomize bases and overlays to generate a final manifest before applying"
    C: "Deletes all resources defined in the directory"
    D: "Creates a new namespace named after the directory"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "-k triggers Kustomize, not a raw file apply."
    B: "-k tells kubectl to run Kustomize on the directory, producing a combined manifest that is then applied. (https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/references/kustomize/kustomization/)"
    C: "Deletion requires 'kubectl delete'."
    D: "Namespace creation is explicit with a manifest."
